{"schema_version": 1, "ok": true, "result": {"schema_version": 1, "version": "0.3.0", "protocol": "MaaFramework ProjectInterface V2", "topic": "project", "content": "An ordinary MaaFramework handoff has interface.json beside its resource directory. interface_version is the number 2. resource is an array of named bundles; each path is an array of project-relative directories, loaded in order. task is an array of exposed tasks: name is the task ID, label is optional display text, and entry is the exact Pipeline node name to start, not a file path or a list. The entry must exist in the selected resource/pipeline/*.json or *.jsonc; task names and entry names may differ. Keep existing declarations and add the intended task instead of replacing unrelated tasks or resources. The example declares an Android Adb controller, matching init; choose the intended client's supported controller. vibe-maa's browser backend uses a CustomController adapter. vibe-maa browser/demo sessions are separate transports, not new ProjectInterface controller types. A browser-tested resource still needs a suitable browser controller integration when distributed to another runner. dev/tasks/*.json is vibe-maa's development acceptance ledger; its goal, cases and max_attempts do not belong in interface.json task items. Declaring or exporting an entry does not prove it works: run current source and retain evidence for the configured goal.", "examples": {"interface.json": {"interface_version": 2, "name": "sample-project", "controller": [{"name": "Android", "type": "Adb"}], "resource": [{"name": "Default", "path": ["./resource"]}], "task": [{"name": "MainTask", "label": "Run task", "entry": "TaskEntry"}]}}, "layout": {"interface.json": "ProjectInterface metadata and public task entries", "resource/pipeline/*.json": "Pipeline nodes, including the declared TaskEntry", "resource/image/": "Observed templates referenced by Pipeline recognition", "dev/tasks/*.json": "Development acceptance contracts, separate from public tasks", "dev/cases/": "Labeled screenshots and recognition case manifests"}, "commands": {"initialize": "vibe-maa --project PATH init", "inspect": "vibe-maa --project PATH context", "pipeline_contract": "vibe-maa reference --topic pipeline", "export": "vibe-maa --project PATH export --output project.zip"}, "source": "https://github.com/MaaXYZ/MaaFramework/blob/main/docs/en_us/3.3-ProjectInterfaceV2.md"}}

--- pipeline ---
{"schema_version": 1, "ok": true, "result": {"schema_version": 1, "version": "0.3.0", "protocol": "MaaFramework 5.16 core subset", "topic": "pipeline", "content": "Write a JSON/JSONC object mapping unique node names to node objects in resource/pipeline/*.json. Images belong in resource/image/; optional OCR files belong in resource/model/ocr/. All coordinates use the returned controller screenshot pixels. Use a DirectHit + DoNothing entry with next candidates for a predictable dispatcher. In the verified 5.16 runtime an entry with conditional recognition is also recognized before its action, so an entry may wait or fail without dispatching input. MaaFW checks next in order on each frame; the first matching candidate executes its action, then follows that candidate's next. An empty next ends that path. After a node runs, its timeout controls waiting for its NEXT list: change the predecessor timeout to extend that candidate wait. Initial entry recognition uses the entry's own timeout. timeout, rate_limit, pre_delay and post_delay are milliseconds; the vibe-maa run --timeout-seconds budget is seconds. Use bounded waits. on_error handles action failure or no next match before timeout. Verify the goal separately using observable recognition; DirectHit is not a valid completion check.", "examples": {"model_free_click_flow": {"Start": {"recognition": "DirectHit", "action": "DoNothing", "next": ["ClickBlack"], "timeout": 1500, "rate_limit": 20}, "ClickBlack": {"recognition": "ColorMatch", "roi": [0, 0, 16, 16], "lower": [0, 0, 0], "upper": [5, 5, 5], "count": 100, "action": "Click", "target": [20, 20], "next": ["WhiteVisible"], "timeout": 1500, "rate_limit": 20}, "WhiteVisible": {"recognition": "ColorMatch", "roi": [0, 0, 16, 16], "lower": [250, 250, 250], "upper": [255, 255, 255], "count": 100, "action": "DoNothing"}}}, "example_setup": "The model-free example expects a black 64x64 image and a target that turns white after clicking [20,20]. Entry: Start. Goal: WhiteVisible. Adapt observations to the real UI.", "commands": {"inspect": "vibe-maa --project PATH observe --session SESSION_ID", "probe": "vibe-maa --project PATH probe --session SESSION_ID --observation OBS_ID --node NODE", "run": "vibe-maa --project PATH run --session SESSION_ID --entry Start --goal WhiteVisible --timeout-seconds 10"}}}

{"schema_version": 1, "ok": true, "result": {"schema_version": 1, "version": "0.3.0", "protocol": "MaaFramework 5.16 core subset", "topic": "actions", "content": "Use exact native action names: DoNothing, Click, Swipe, ClickKey, InputText. Click target defaults to true: the just-matched box; target [x,y] is a fixed point; [x,y,width,height] is a region, with a sampled click location. Numeric coordinates must come from observations. Swipe uses begin/end and duration in milliseconds. ClickKey uses numeric key, not key_name; InputText uses input_text, not text. Key codes depend on the controller: browser/Win32 VK 113 is F2, while Android 4 is Back. Browser supports its documented subset of VK codes and no DOM selectors; some native controllers limit text to ASCII. Observe again after input. CLI act uses its own type/key/text fields and always needs a fresh observation; key/text inputs also need a target or guard box. Pipeline field names and CLI arguments differ.", "examples": {"click_matched_node": {"recognition": "OCR", "expected": "Confirm", "action": "Click", "target": true}, "browser_or_win32_f2": {"recognition": "DirectHit", "action": "ClickKey", "key": 113}, "android_back": {"recognition": "DirectHit", "action": "ClickKey", "key": 4}, "type_text": {"recognition": "DirectHit", "action": "InputText", "input_text": "Sample label"}, "swipe": {"recognition": "DirectHit", "action": "Swipe", "begin": [200, 300], "end": [200, 100], "duration": 300}}, "commands": {"click": "vibe-maa --project PATH act --session SESSION_ID --observation OBS_ID --target e2", "key": "vibe-maa --project PATH act --session SESSION_ID --observation OBS_ID --type key --key 113 --box 0,0,100,100"}}}

--- recognition ---
{"schema_version": 1, "ok": true, "result": {"schema_version": 1, "version": "0.3.0", "protocol": "MaaFramework 5.16 core subset", "topic": "recognition", "content": "roi is [x,y,width,height], not two corner points. Prefer a small stable region with positive and negative examples. TemplateMatch uses template relative to resource/image/ and threshold in [0,1]. Crop an observed original with asset extract before using it; keep the same screenshot scale. OCR uses expected text or regex and requires det.onnx, rec.onnx and keys.txt in resource/model/ocr/. Missing OCR is not an empty-screen result. ColorMatch uses lower/upper bounds; its default method 4 uses RGB values, although adapter arrays internally use BGR. count is the minimum matching pixel count. DirectHit is unconditional. Probe uses saved pixels and never runs action/next; inspect result.hit, since nonempty all_results can still fail filtering. Offline probes reject Custom, And/Or and context-dependent ROI. A DirectHit + DoNothing entry with guarded next candidates clearly separates dispatch from UI-dependent actions.", "examples": {"template_node": {"recognition": "TemplateMatch", "template": "confirm.png", "roi": [100, 100, 300, 200], "threshold": 0.85, "action": "Click"}, "ocr_node": {"recognition": "OCR", "expected": "^Confirm$", "roi": [100, 100, 300, 200], "action": "Click"}}, "commands": {"crop": "vibe-maa --project PATH asset extract --observation OBS_ID --box 100,100,150,40 --name confirm", "check_cases": "vibe-maa --project PATH case check --session SESSION_ID --cases dev/cases/checks.json"}}}

